nswer.type/math :answer/value "4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-43f51336-4d87-5e3a-8c17-d2fcf2b0a3a3" :question/id #uuid "43f51336-4d87-5e3a-8c17-d2fcf2b0a3a3" :question/math-academy-id "q-268179" :question/problem "Consider the functions $y:{R}^{3}\\to {R}^{2}$ and $f:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2},$ defined by\n\n$$\ny(x)=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}{x}_{2}{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}\\end{aligned}].\\,f(y)=[\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}+{y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}{y}_{2}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nAccording to the chain rule, the derivative \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}f(y(x))\n$$\n\nis the product of a$\\,\\,${{field-1}}$\\times ${{field-2}} \nmatrix with a \n{{field-3}} $\\times $ {{field-4}} \nmatrix, which results in a \n{{field-5}} $\\times $ {{field-6}} \nmatrix." :question/worked-solution "Using the chain rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}f(y(x))=\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nLet's consider each of the factors in turn.\n\n- The function $f$ acts on a vector $y\\in {R}^{2}$ and outputs a vector $f(y)\\in {R}^{2}.$ Thus, the total derivative of $f$ with respect to $y$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\n- The function $y$ acts on a vector $x\\in {R}^{3}$ and outputs a vector $y(x)\\in {R}^{2}.$ Thus, the total derivative of $y$ with respect to $x$ is a $2\\times 3$ matrix.\n\nTherefore, the product\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\n$$\n\nof a $2\\times 2$ matrix with a $2\\times 3$ matrix is a $2\\times 3$ matrix." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-06e8af08-6f3d-542f-9c27-ac0092d1e952" "answer-field-3b10feee-49bf-5f48-a752-82ca217efd7c" "answer-field-ce46bf49-6669-5133-a050-5509d649b2d1" "answer-field-6f00946b-80d3-591e-9f55-4d7a50d7a967" "answer-field-37608c1b-6b71-5eee-be39-b1d5820e3583" "answer-field-273ae1bc-6569-51f3-9b52-35e60079ae43"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/